# Hand-written deepening modules per topic (own wording). Appended after the 8 template modules.
# Each: (title, bloom, hours, intro, [points], example, activity, standard)
D = {
"petroleum": [
 ("Read a process flow and a permit","Analyse",8,"Every job on a hydrocarbon site starts with two papers: the flow diagram that shows where the product goes, and the permit that says who may do what.",
  ["On a flow diagram, trace one product from tank to dispatch and name each vessel, pump, valve and instrument on the way.","A permit states the job, place, time limit, hazards, isolations and the signatures of issuer and receiver. A permit with a blank line is not valid.","Gas tests come before hot work and are repeated at set intervals; write down each reading with time and tester.","Handover between shifts must name open permits, isolated equipment and any abnormal readings."],
  "A pump seal job: isolate and drain, gas test 0% of the lower limit, permit signed 07:10, expires 15:00. At 12:30 the wind changes and a test reads 8%; work stops and the permit is suspended until a fresh test is clear.",
  "Trace a flow diagram supplied by your mentor and fill a sample permit for a pump seal change, naming every isolation.","Every vessel and valve on the route named; permit has no blank lines; stop-work trigger stated."),
 ("Measure, balance and account for oil","Apply",10,"Oil is sold by measured quantity, so measurement accuracy is money. A balance compares what came in with what went out.",
  ["Quantity depends on temperature: volume at observed temperature is corrected to a standard temperature before it is compared.","Check the tank gauge, a manual dip and the flow meter against one another; differences beyond the allowed band are investigated, not averaged away.","Mass balance: opening stock plus receipts minus dispatches minus closing stock should equal the loss, and the loss should be small and explained.","Record each custody transfer with the time, meter readings, seal numbers and both signatures."],
  "Opening stock 40,000 barrels, receipts 12,000, dispatches 11,700, closing stock 40,150. Loss = 40,000 + 12,000 - 11,700 - 40,150 = 150 barrels, 0.29% of throughput. Within a 0.5% band, so log it and note the cause.",
  "Complete a three-day mass balance from sample data and explain any gap above 0.2%.","Arithmetic checked twice; loss percent stated; cause or next check named for every gap."),
 ("Abnormal situations and escalation","Evaluate",9,"Most incidents begin as small deviations that nobody wrote down. The skill is to notice early and escalate clearly.",
  ["Know alarm priorities: act on the highest first, never silence an alarm without a note.","Use a short report format: what, where, when, how bad, what is done, what is needed.","Practice three drills on paper: small leak, power loss, high tank level. For each write the first three actions in order.","Review near-misses weekly; the point is to learn, not to blame."],
  "A high-level alarm sounds at 82% on a tank due to reach 90% in 40 minutes. Actions: confirm with a manual dip, stop the incoming transfer, tell the control room, log the time and decision.",
  "Write the first three actions for each of the three drills and have your supervisor mark them.","Actions in a safe order; reports use the six-part format; a near-miss review note is filed."),
],
"petrochemicals": [
 ("From feedstock to product: the reaction chain","Understand",8,"Plants turn simple gases and liquids into building blocks, and then into plastics. Knowing the chain explains why one upset affects many products.",
  ["Draw the chain from feedstock to a final plastic and name each intermediate.","Heat, pressure and a catalyst decide how much product forms; each has a safe window.","Side products are not always waste: some are sold or burned for energy.","A change in feed quality changes everything downstream, so feed is tested on arrival."],
  "A feed lot arrives with more water than allowed. The lab flags it, the lot is held, and the plant uses stock from another tank instead of risking the catalyst.",
  "Draw the chain for one plastic and mark where heat, pressure and a catalyst act.","Every step labelled; each safe window written; feed test named."),
 ("Runaway reactions and pressure safety","Apply",10,"A reaction that makes its own heat can speed up faster than cooling can follow. Pressure relief and interlocks exist for that moment.",
  ["Know the signs: rising temperature with cooling at maximum, pressure rising faster than normal.","A relief valve opens at a set pressure and sends gas to a safe place; it must never be isolated without a permit.","An interlock stops the feed automatically if a limit is crossed; never bypass one without written approval.","Practise the shutdown order on paper: stop feed, keep cooling, vent safely, call for help."],
  "Reactor temperature climbs 3 degrees per minute with cooling full. At the alarm limit the interlock stops the feed. The operator confirms, keeps cooling and reports the time to the supervisor.",
  "Write the shutdown order for a reactor and list which devices act at each step.","Order is safe; no step relies on bypassing a protection."),
 ("Quality of plastics: tests and records","Evaluate",9,"Customers buy by property: how easily the plastic melts, how dense it is, how strong. Each lot carries a test certificate.",
  ["Typical tests: melt flow, density, tensile strength, colour and moisture.","Sample the same way every time: from several places, labelled and sealed.","Compare results with the written specification; out-of-limit lots are held, retested and decided by the quality lead.","Keep records for each lot so a complaint can be traced to the exact shift."],
  "Lot 114 melt flow is 8.4 against a limit of 7.0 to 8.0. It is held; a retest gives 8.3; the lot is sold as a lower grade with the customer told.",
  "Fill a certificate for three sample lots and decide each as pass, hold or downgrade.","Every result compared with a limit; decisions explained."),
],
"aluminum": [
 ("Electricity is the main ingredient","Analyse",8,"Making metal from its oxide uses large electric current day and night, so power cost and stability shape everything.",
  ["Energy per tonne is the key cost number; track it daily.","A power dip can freeze a production cell, so planned power cuts are rare and well warned.","Efficiency is the share of current that actually makes metal; losses show up as heat.","Heat is useful but dangerous: cell surfaces and spilled metal burn at long range."],
  "A cell uses 14,000 units of electricity per tonne against a target of 13,500. The 500 extra units cost AED 0.2 each, so each tonne costs AED 100 more than planned.",
  "Calculate energy cost per tonne for a week of sample data and mark the days above target.","Units shown; AED cost per tonne correct; days above target explained."),
 ("Casting safely: water and molten metal","Apply",10,"Water meeting molten metal can explode. Every mould, tool and charge must be dry.",
  ["Preheat tools and moulds; check for damp before each pour.","Wear the full rated clothing: face shield, heat-proof coat, boots over trouser legs.","Keep a clear escape path and a second person watching.","Skim dross and weigh it; dross is lost metal and a measure of care."],
  "A damp ladle is found on the preheat rack. It is tagged, set aside and the pour is delayed by 20 minutes. Delay costs less than an incident.",
  "Write a dry-check list for a casting shift and mark who signs each item.","Every item has a signer; escape path drawn."),
 ("Alloy, grade and customer needs","Evaluate",9,"Customers do not buy 'aluminium'; they buy a grade with stated composition and strength.",
  ["Know that small additions change the metal: strength, flow, finish.","Each cast is sampled and the composition tested before release.","Out-of-grade metal is recycled into a lower grade or remelted.","Match the grade to the end use: facade profile, car part or foil."],
  "A cast shows more iron than allowed for a facade grade. It is sold for a less demanding use at a lower price, with the certificate showing the actual composition.",
  "Match five end uses to grades from a list and explain each match.","Matches justified by composition or strength."),
],
"cement": [
 ("Raw mix, burning and the heart of the plant","Understand",8,"Limestone, clay and small correctors are ground and blended, then heated in a rotating kiln until they fuse.",
  ["Blend consistency matters; test the mix every hour and adjust the feed.","Kiln temperature is high and steady; sudden changes damage the lining.","Fuel can include alternative fuels, which need strict control of what is burned.","Heat leaving the kiln is recovered to dry raw materials."],
  "The hourly test shows lime content slipping. The operator raises the limestone feed by 1% and retests in an hour.",
  "Draw the plant from quarry to kiln and mark each test point.","Test points marked; adjustment rule written."),
 ("Dust, noise and heat: working safely","Apply",9,"Cement plants are dusty and hot. Controls are filters, enclosures and clear routines.",
  ["Dust masks are the last control; first use enclosed conveyors and filters.","Never enter a silo or hopper without a permit; material can collapse like quicksand.","Hearing protection is required near mills and fans.","Drink water and rest in shade in summer heat; know the signs of heat illness."],
  "A blocked chute tempts a worker to climb in. The permit rule says no; the plant stops the feed, locks it out, and uses an air cannon from outside.",
  "List five confined-space rules and the reason for each.","Each rule has a reason; lockout step named."),
 ("Strength, fineness and what builders need","Evaluate",9,"Builders ask for strength at 7 and 28 days and for workable mixes. The mill setting decides the fineness.",
  ["Finer grinding gives faster strength but costs more power.","Gypsum controls how fast the cement sets.","Test cubes are made, cured and crushed on set days.","Bulk deliveries need clean tankers to avoid mixing grades."],
  "A 28-day cube breaks at 47 MPa against a class minimum of 42.5. Pass, with a margin of 4.5 MPa.",
  "Fill a results table for six sample cubes and decide pass or fail.","Margins calculated; decisions justified."),
],
"fertilizer": [
 ("Nutrients and crop needs","Understand",8,"Crops need nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium in different amounts at different stages. A grade like 20-10-10 states the percentage of each.",
  ["Read a grade and work out kilograms of each nutrient in a 50 kg bag.","Sandy desert soils hold little water and nutrients, so timing and split doses matter.","Over-use wastes money and can harm water; match dose to a soil test.","Slow-release products cost more but last longer."],
  "A 50 kg bag of 20-10-10 holds 10 kg nitrogen, 5 kg phosphorus and 5 kg potassium.",
  "Calculate nutrient weights for three bag grades.","Arithmetic correct; units shown."),
 ("Granulation, drying and storage hazards","Apply",10,"Granules spread evenly and store better than powder, but some fertilisers can decompose or burn if stored carelessly.",
  ["Keep stores dry, ventilated and away from fuels and heat.","Never mix incompatible products; follow the segregation chart.","Dust can be explosive in air; control it.","Know the first-aid and spill steps from the safety data sheet."],
  "A damaged bag is found leaking beside a pallet of another product. It is cleaned up with dry methods, the area is ventilated and both pallets are moved apart.",
  "Draw a store layout with segregation and fire routes.","Incompatible items separated; routes clear."),
 ("Quality: size, moisture and caking","Evaluate",9,"A good granule is the right size, dry, and strong enough to survive transport.",
  ["Screen samples to find the share within the target size.","Moisture above the limit leads to lumps and weaker bags.","Add anti-caking coatings where specified.","Record each lot with date, grade and test results."],
  "A sieve test shows 91% of granules in range against a 90% target. Pass with a thin margin; the operator checks the screen for wear.",
  "Fill a quality sheet for four sample lots and decide each.","All fields completed; decisions explained."),
],
}

